WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.200 --> 00:00:01.760
Hello. I'm Alice Oseman.

00:00:01.840 --> 00:00:04.640
I'm the writer and creator
of Heartstopper.

00:00:12.600 --> 00:00:14.800
Slate 1,000. Take one.

00:00:15.520 --> 00:00:18.600
The show is a little bit
different to the comic

00:00:18.680 --> 00:00:21.440
and I like to think of it
in that

00:00:21.520 --> 00:00:25.240
the TV show is an expanded
version of the comic.

00:00:25.320 --> 00:00:26.960
The core story of
Heartstopper is

00:00:27.040 --> 00:00:28.640
very much in the show.

00:00:28.720 --> 00:00:31.160
We haven't changed
what is in the comic.

00:00:31.240 --> 00:00:33.600
We've just made
the story bigger.

00:00:33.680 --> 00:00:35.640
We've made the world wider.

00:00:35.720 --> 00:00:37.000
Beyond Nick and Charlie's
story,

00:00:37.080 --> 00:00:39.520
we learn a lot more about
Tao and Elle

00:00:39.600 --> 00:00:40.520
and Tara and Darcy.

00:00:40.600 --> 00:00:42.760
And there are also
new characters as well.

00:00:42.840 --> 00:00:45.360
The thing that I've learnt
about adaptations is that

00:00:45.440 --> 00:00:48.400
something that works for
one format, like a comic,

00:00:48.480 --> 00:00:50.560
might not work for
a TV show.

00:00:50.640 --> 00:00:53.840
So, when developing
Heartstopper as a TV show,

00:00:53.920 --> 00:00:56.560
we had to look at it
from a TV perspective

00:00:56.640 --> 00:00:58.920
and think
how do we take this story

00:00:59.000 --> 00:01:00.760
and make it work on screen?

00:01:00.840 --> 00:01:02.440
And that's been part of
the fun of it for me,

00:01:02.520 --> 00:01:04.360
just expanding the story

00:01:04.440 --> 00:01:06.480
to make it
a really cool TV show.

00:01:06.560 --> 00:01:09.680
Seeing the characters
for the first time was

00:01:09.760 --> 00:01:11.000
so surreal.

00:01:11.080 --> 00:01:13.280
It was probably
during rehearsals

00:01:13.360 --> 00:01:16.000
when we first saw them
all together

00:01:16.080 --> 00:01:17.800
in, like,
the Heartstopper gang

00:01:17.880 --> 00:01:19.920
and it was just very weird.

00:01:20.720 --> 00:01:21.960
You know,
they're all amazing

00:01:22.040 --> 00:01:24.040
and it's just been
wonderful.

00:01:24.120 --> 00:01:26.160
We're there
if they have any problems

00:01:26.240 --> 00:01:28.840
or anything, you know, that
they want to talk about,

00:01:28.920 --> 00:01:30.760
just anything to do with
the show.

00:01:30.840 --> 00:01:33.040
I mean,
they're all so talented.

00:01:33.120 --> 00:01:34.280
They just are
the characters.

00:01:34.360 --> 00:01:36.000
We're just there
to support them

00:01:36.080 --> 00:01:38.000
and see them thrive.

00:01:38.760 --> 00:01:41.120
So, my day-to-day experience
on set,

00:01:41.200 --> 00:01:42.680
because I've been here
every day…

00:01:42.760 --> 00:01:44.840
Basically me and Patrick,

00:01:44.920 --> 00:01:46.240
one of
the executive producers,

00:01:46.320 --> 00:01:48.080
we watch all the scenes.

00:01:48.160 --> 00:01:50.480
We talk to Euros,
the director.

00:01:50.560 --> 00:01:53.680
We sort of share ideas about
how things should be filmed

00:01:53.760 --> 00:01:55.800
and we're also
in consultation with

00:01:55.880 --> 00:01:57.200
all the
different departments,

00:01:57.280 --> 00:02:00.160
like costume and the
art department and props.

00:02:00.240 --> 00:02:03.360
And I kind of feel like
it's my role to

00:02:03.440 --> 00:02:05.760
represent the comic
and the readers

00:02:05.840 --> 00:02:08.560
and I think that
we have achieved that.

00:02:08.640 --> 00:02:11.640
So, I had never been on
a TV set before.

00:02:11.720 --> 00:02:15.040
I honestly didn't know
anything about TV making

00:02:15.120 --> 00:02:15.960
before this.

00:02:16.040 --> 00:02:18.280
And I've just learnt so much
about

00:02:18.360 --> 00:02:20.200
what everyone on set does,

00:02:20.280 --> 00:02:22.640
all the different roles
that there are.

00:02:22.720 --> 00:02:25.880
And it really is
a team effort, which is…

00:02:25.960 --> 00:02:27.240
You know, I'm a writer.

00:02:27.320 --> 00:02:28.880
I'm used to working
by myself.

00:02:28.960 --> 00:02:31.240
But this has, you know,

00:02:31.320 --> 00:02:33.520
really highlighted what
you can do when there are

00:02:33.600 --> 00:02:36.120
so many different
talented people involved.

00:02:36.200 --> 00:02:39.080
I want Heartstopper to
inspire young people,

00:02:39.160 --> 00:02:41.520
especially
young LGBTQ+ people,

00:02:41.600 --> 00:02:43.320
to be
whoever they want to be

00:02:43.400 --> 00:02:45.760
and to believe that
they can find happiness

00:02:45.840 --> 00:02:47.520
and find love
and find friendship.

00:02:47.600 --> 00:02:49.280
Because it is
a joyful story,

00:02:49.840 --> 00:02:52.320
everyone can get something
out of it, so…

